course: biology
lesson: 226
title: Kindness to wild animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Walking through a meadow, a family finds a baby deer curled up alone in the long grass and wants to rescue it, but its mother is almost certainly nearby, feeding, and the kindest thing is to walk quietly away.
explain: Being kind to wild animals often means leaving them alone: a frog on a path can be gently moved aside with wet hands or not touched at all, because soap and sun cream on our skin can harm its thin skin, and an ant nest should not be poked just for fun.
explain: Our rubbish can hurt animals too: swans get tangled in old fishing line, and ducks and birds get caught in plastic drink rings, so we take line home and cut the rings before throwing them away.
explain: A shallow dish of water in hot weather helps birds, bees and hedgehogs, and an injured bird needs a wildlife rescue, not a cardboard box at home.
explain: Animals feel pain and fear as we do, so throwing stones at a cat, keeping a frog in a jar or leaving a dog chained up alone all day are cruel, even if nobody means harm.
fact: A baby deer, called a fawn, is often left hidden in the grass while its mother feeds nearby, so it should be left alone.
fact: Frogs breathe partly through their thin, moist skin, which can be hurt by soap, sun cream and dry hands; it is best not to pick them up.
fact: Ants defend their nest when it is poked; small creatures matter and help the world work, by cleaning up and mixing the soil.
fact: Fishing line left on a riverbank tangles swans, ducks and other birds and can badly hurt them.
fact: Animals can get their heads or beaks caught in plastic drink rings, so cutting the rings before throwing them away helps.
fact: A shallow dish of water in hot weather helps birds, bees and hedgehogs; a stone in it lets insects climb out.
fact: Animals feel pain and fear like us, so hurting them on purpose is wrong.
fact: If you find an injured wild bird, tell an adult who can call a wildlife rescue, because wild animals need special care.
fact: If a bat flies into a house, open the windows wide, turn off the lights and leave the room; the bat usually finds its own way out, and it should not be touched with bare hands.
fact: Wild animals belong in the wild; a jar is too hot, dry and small for a frog.
fact: Dogs are social animals and need company; a dog left chained alone all day becomes lonely and unhappy, and an adult can report it to an animal welfare charity.
fact: Hedgehogs must never be given cow's milk, which upsets their stomachs; water is best.
exercise: You find a baby deer alone in the grass. What should you do? | Walk quietly away, its mother is probably nearby | Carry it home and feed it milk from a bottle | Stay next to it until its mother comes | Take it to the nearest farm
exercise: Why should you not handle a frog with sun cream on your hands? | Its thin skin can be harmed by the cream | The cream will make it jump far too high | Frogs hate the smell and bite | The cream will slowly turn the frog's green skin white
exercise: What do ants do when someone pokes their nest? | They rush out to defend it | They always move house at once | They fall asleep from the shock | They welcome the visitor with food
exercise: Why does it matter if small creatures like ants are hurt? | They help the world work, cleaning up and mixing soil | It does not matter at all, they are too small to feel | Ants are the only food for elephants | Ants control the weather
exercise: Why is fishing line left on a riverbank dangerous? | Swans and ducks get tangled and hurt | It pollutes the water with ink | It scares the fish away for ever and ever | It makes the grass stop growing
exercise: What should you do with plastic rings from a drinks pack? | Cut them before throwing them away | Throw them in the river for the fish | Hang them on trees for birds to play with | Bury them in the garden
exercise: Why put out a shallow dish of water in hot weather? | Birds, bees and hedgehogs need water | To stop it raining on the garden | To catch flies for the cat to eat | To make the garden look pretty
exercise: Why put a stone in the water dish? | So bees and insects can climb out | To keep the water cold all day | To stop the birds drinking | To make the water taste better to the birds
exercise: Is it all right to throw stones at a stray cat? | No, animals feel pain and fear like us | Yes, if it is not your own cat | Yes, cats always land safely | Yes, as long as the stones are small ones
exercise: You find an injured bird in the garden. What is the best thing to do? | Tell an adult who can call a wildlife rescue | Keep it in a box and feed it bread | Put it high up in a tree, away from cats | Throw it into the air to make it fly
exercise: A bat flies into your bedroom. What should you do? | Open the windows, turn off the lights and leave it | Catch it with your bare hands | Turn on all the lights to help it see the way out | Shut the window so it stays in
exercise: Why is a jar a bad home for a frog? | It is too hot, dry and small | It is too wet and cold | Frogs prefer cardboard boxes | Frogs need a jar with a lid only
exercise: Why is a dog left chained up alone all day unhappy? | Dogs need company and become lonely | Dogs prefer being alone all day | Chains keep dogs warm and calm | Dogs sleep all day anyway, so they do not notice
exercise: What should a hedgehog visiting the garden be given to drink? | Water | Cow's milk | Lemonade | Cold tea
test: «found a baby deer all alone in the field, poor thing, let's take it home!!» What is right? | No, its mother is probably feeding nearby; leave it | Yes, it has been abandoned for ever | Yes, and give it cow's milk from a bottle every hour | No, deer babies live alone all their lives
test: Count them: 4 children each catch 2 frogs and put them in jars. How many frogs need to go back to the pond? | 8 | 6 | 4 | 2
test: «just poking the ants nest with a stick lol, they're only ants» What is the best answer? | Leave them; even small creatures matter and feel threatened | Fine, ants feel nothing at all | Fine, it helps the ants get exercise | Poke harder so they all come out to play with you in the sun
test: In a famous cartoon a fawn's mother is lost, so people think every fawn alone needs rescue. What is true in real life? | A fawn alone is usually hidden while its mother feeds nearby | Every fawn alone has lost its mother for ever | Fawns are always left alone at birth for ever | Fawns live with rabbits, not with their mothers, until they are grown
test: «the fishing line's tangled, just chuck it in the bushes» What could happen? | Birds like swans could get tangled and hurt | The line would grow into a new plant | The fish would use it to make their nests | Nothing, because birds never touch fishing line
test: «hedgehog in the garden, give it a saucer of milk» What is best? | Give it water; cow's milk upsets its stomach | Give it milk, hedgehogs love it | Give it lemonade for energy | Give it nothing, because hedgehogs never drink
test: What would happen if a child kept a frog in a jar on a sunny windowsill? | The frog would get too hot and dry and could die | The frog would grow bigger and happier | The frog would turn into a toad | Nothing, frogs like hot glass and lots of bright sunshine
test: «bat in the house!! grab it with a towel and chuck it out?» What is safest? | Open the windows, turn off the lights and let it fly out | Grab it in your hands and throw it | Hit it with a broom | Put all the lights on so that it can see the door and fly out
test: «there's a dog chained in next door's yard all day every day, none of my business tho» What is kindest? | Tell an adult, who can contact an animal welfare charity | Climb over and set it free | Throw it food over the fence | Ignore it, dogs love being alone
test: «ok the bird's hurt, let's keep it in my room and feed it bread» | Better tell an adult to call a wildlife rescue | Yes, bread will make it better | Yes, birds like living in bedrooms | Put it out on the road so it can fly
test: Count them: a summer water dish is visited by 3 blackbirds and 2 hedgehogs. How many legs came to drink? | 14 | 10 | 20 | 12
exam: Identify the appropriate course of action upon the discovery of a solitary fawn concealed in long grass. | The fawn should be left undisturbed, since the mother is probably feeding nearby | The fawn should be removed to a domestic dwelling and fed with cow's milk | The observer should remain beside the fawn until the arrival of the mother, however long | The fawn should be conveyed to the nearest agricultural holding
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard to wildlife presented by discarded fishing line? | Birds such as swans and ducks may become entangled and sustain injury | The water is contaminated by dyes released from the line | Fish are permanently deterred from the watercourse in which the line lies | The growth of bankside vegetation is thereby prevented
exam: Select the response consistent with the established guidance in respect of a bat which has entered a dwelling. | The windows should be opened and the lights extinguished, so that the bat may depart | The bat should be captured in the bare hands and released | All lights should be illuminated so that the bat may perceive the exit and depart | The windows should be closed so that the bat may be retained
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a glass jar is unsuitable as accommodation for a wild frog. | The jar is excessively hot, dry and confined for the animal | The jar is excessively cold and damp for the animal | Frogs are unable to perceive the walls of a glass vessel | The jar is suitable provided that a lid is fitted
